  "answer": "It has been proposed that organizations empirically measure and objectively evaluate the effects of interventions on both safety metrics and general capabilities metrics together (https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12001, https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/wpsGprQCRffRKG92v/catastrophic-risks-from-ai-4-organizational-risks). \n\nThe mechanism is that general capability advances often improve specific safety metrics without actually reducing overall AI risk (https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12001, https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/wpsGprQCRffRKG92v/catastrophic-risks-from-ai-4-organizational-risks). By contrasting empirical measurements of both safety and capabilities, organizations can determine whether safety has improved relative to general capabilities, rather than relying on intuition, gut feeling, or appeals to authority (https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12001, https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/wpsGprQCRffRKG92v/catastrophic-risks-from-ai-4-organizational-risks). This helps prevent \"safetywashing\"—the misrepresentation of capability developments or ineffective procedures as safety improvements—and ensures that the research yields genuine progress in reducing overall risk rather than a lack of meaningful progress or the inadvertent acceleration of AI development (https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12001, https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/wpsGprQCRffRKG92v/catastrophic-risks-from-ai-4-organizational-risks)."
}
